  {
    "episode_id": 66265,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/6/6/66265.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 21,
    "turn_text": "I did 26 years in the military. Over 21 of those as a Navy SEAL. Out of those 20 years, I spent about 16 years away from home. That's training and deployed. I deployed eight times for six months or longer. And five of those were to combat. I've lived and worked on five of the seven continents. My first combat tours in Bosnia Herzegovina when I was 26 years old and then I went to Afghanistan Iraq Afghanistan and Back to Iraq and Horn of Africa and all over the place. It's very interesting work Our nation now we seem to be Thank God pulling back from these forever wars, but when your country requires your service, that's what you do. And a Navy SEAL's natural environment is combat. So you should, if you're in that position, or the men and women that are serving now, in the military, they should be striving every single day to go to combat to help protect our nation. It's not for everybody, that's just the way it is, but if it is for you, you're capable of accomplishing those tasks and you take it upon yourself to volunteer to be in the military. You should be focusing as much as you can to do the best that you can everywhere. I did this interview, it was fascinating with these guys over at Amman, Jordan. There's like 30 sailors who did a Zoom call just to talk about leadership and how things have changed and what they haven't, how they've not changed. These are all folks that are prepping to go into the sandbox, we call it. to move even farther forward. And it was really interesting to see these younger sailors now, they remind me of when I was that age, but they have kind of a different view on life because they joined the military in a time of war. I joined the military in a time of peace. So they were much more, I think, apt to understand exactly what the heck they're getting into than I was at that time in 1988.",
